TG-DSC is used to track the mass change and thermal effects of C-LFS during heating. The main goal is to identify the decomposition weight-loss ranges of carbonate phases (e.g., CaCO 3  and MgCO 3 ). Based on these ranges, the released CO 2  is back-calculated. This allows the CO 2  uptake and fixation during carbonation to be quantified. Before testing, a 1 cm 3  piece is taken from the interior of the specimen with a freshly broken surface. It is immediately crushed and ground to pass a 75 μm sieve. The powder is dried to constant mass at 60 °C under vacuum (or in a drying oven) and then sealed for storage. For each run, about 10–20 mg of powder is placed in a crucible. The sample is heated to 900–1000 °C at 10 °C/min under a nitrogen atmosphere. TG and DSC curves are recorded at the same time. For the representative mixture used in the carbonation-age comparison, the baseline carbonate contribution from SP was internally corrected using the non-carbonated (0 day) specimen of the same composition. Therefore, the additional mass loss in the 700–850 ℃ interval relative to the 0-day control was interpreted as carbonation-induced extra CaCO 3  accumulation. The calculation formula is shown in Eq. ( 1 ). TG-DSC analysis was performed using a ZH-1150Q thermal analyzer (Beijing Zhonglu Instrument Co., Ltd., China). 1 Δ m C a C O 3 , e x t r a t = m 700 - 850 t - m 700 - 850 0 d \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
